WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.696
We begin by distinguishing how signals exist
in time.

2
00:00:05.696 --> 00:00:14.567
A continuous signal varies smoothly without
interruption, like the voltage across a

3
00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:16.785
capacitor charging gradually.

4
00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:24.214
Mathematically, we describe it with a function
that accepts any real number for time.

5
00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:32.392
A discrete signal, however, only exists at
specific, separate moments.

6
00:00:32.392 --> 00:00:40.980
Think of a metronome ticking or a digital
audio file recording sound only at fixed

7
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:41.553
intervals.

8
00:00:41.553 --> 00:00:50.052
The key difference lies in the domain:
continuous signals use real-valued time, while

9
00:00:50.052 --> 00:00:53.321
discrete signals use integer indices.

10
00:00:53.321 --> 00:01:01.777
This distinction shapes how we analyze, store,
and process information in engineering

11
00:01:01.777 --> 00:01:02.482
systems.

12
00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:10.679
Understanding this boundary is essential
before we model how systems respond to these

13
00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:12.571
different input types.

14
00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:17.761
Next, we model how systems process these
signals.

15
00:01:17.761 --> 00:01:25.591
A linear time-invariant system behaves
consistently regardless of when you apply an

16
00:01:25.591 --> 00:01:26.243
input.

17
00:01:26.243 --> 00:01:35.116
Linearity means doubling the input doubles the
output, and time-invariance means the system's

18
00:01:35.116 --> 00:01:38.529
behavior doesn't shift over time.

19
00:01:38.529 --> 00:01:45.482
We characterize such systems using
differential equations for continuous domains

20
00:01:45.482 --> 00:01:49.654
or difference equations for discrete domains.

21
00:01:49.654 --> 00:01:56.011
These equations capture how the system's
internal state evolves.

22
00:01:56.011 --> 00:02:03.993
By solving them, we predict output behavior
without simulating every possible input.

23
00:02:03.993 --> 00:02:12.292
This mathematical framework allows us to
analyze complex circuits or communication

24
00:02:12.292 --> 00:02:19.082
channels using fundamental building blocks
rather than trial-and-error experimentation.

25
00:02:19.226 --> 00:02:28.261
To fully understand an LTI system, we examine
its response to a single, instantaneous spike

26
00:02:28.261 --> 00:02:30.068
called an impulse.

27
00:02:30.068 --> 00:02:38.640
This impulse response reveals exactly how the
system reacts to any arbitrary input.

28
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:47.601
When we feed a general signal into the system,
the output emerges from combining the impulse

29
00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:50.961
response with the input through convolution.

30
00:02:50.961 --> 00:02:58.402
Convolution mathematically slides the impulse
response across the input, scales it, and sums

31
00:02:58.402 --> 00:03:01.836
the products at each time point.

32
00:03:01.836 --> 00:03:09.140
This operation captures how past inputs
influence the current output.

33
00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:16.820
By mastering convolution, we can predict
system behavior for complex waveforms without

34
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:24.500
solving differential equations from scratch,
making it a cornerstone of signal processing

35
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:25.140
analysis.

36
00:03:25.298 --> 00:03:32.301
To analyze signals across frequencies, we use
complex exponentials.

37
00:03:32.301 --> 00:03:40.399
These functions oscillate with constant
magnitude while rotating in the complex plane,

38
00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:45.123
making them ideal for representing sinusoidal
waves.

39
00:03:45.123 --> 00:03:54.640
The Fourier representation decomposes any
signal into a sum of these complex

40
00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:58.605
exponentials, revealing its frequency content.

41
00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:07.486
Instead of working with time-domain waveforms,
we shift to the frequency domain where signals

42
00:04:07.486 --> 00:04:10.658
become vectors of complex coefficients.

43
00:04:10.658 --> 00:04:18.712
This transformation simplifies system analysis
because multiplication in the frequency domain

44
00:04:18.712 --> 00:04:23.837
corresponds to convolution in the time domain.

45
00:04:23.837 --> 00:04:32.352
Engineers use this view to design filters and
modulators by manipulating frequency

46
00:04:32.352 --> 00:04:37.319
components directly rather than solving
differential equations.

47
00:04:37.466 --> 00:04:44.950
The frequency response describes how an LTI
system modifies different frequency

48
00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:45.630
components.

49
00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:53.094
It shows both magnitude attenuation and phase
shift across the spectrum.

50
00:04:53.094 --> 00:05:01.874
When a signal passes through a system, each
complex exponential component gets scaled and

51
00:05:01.874 --> 00:05:08.773
rotated according to the system's frequency
response at that specific frequency.

52
00:05:08.773 --> 00:05:17.394
This means filters can selectively amplify or
suppress certain frequencies while preserving

53
00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:18.112
others.

54
00:05:18.112 --> 00:05:26.849
By examining the magnitude plot, we see which
frequencies pass through unchanged, and the

55
00:05:26.849 --> 00:05:29.969
phase plot reveals timing delays.

56
00:05:29.969 --> 00:05:37.924
Consistent predictions between time and
frequency domains confirm our model accurately

57
00:05:37.924 --> 00:05:45.880
captures the system's behavior, allowing
reliable design of audio equalizers or

58
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.326
communication channels.

59
00:05:47.474 --> 00:05:55.924
When we convert a continuous signal to digital
form, we sample it at regular intervals.

60
00:05:55.924 --> 00:06:03.900
This process creates repeated copies of the
original spectrum, spaced by the sampling

61
00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:04.513
frequency.

62
00:06:04.513 --> 00:06:12.424
Each copy represents the same frequency
content but shifted in the frequency domain.

63
00:06:12.424 --> 00:06:19.510
If the original signal contains frequencies
higher than half the sampling rate, these

64
00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:23.870
shifted copies will overlap with the base
spectrum.

65
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:32.352
This overlap makes distinct frequencies
indistinguishable after sampling, a phenomenon

66
00:06:32.352 --> 00:06:34.049
called aliasing.

67
00:06:34.049 --> 00:06:42.517
To prevent this, we must ensure the signal is
bandlimited and apply anti-aliasing filters

68
00:06:42.517 --> 00:06:43.727
before sampling.

69
00:06:43.727 --> 00:06:51.031
Proper sampling preserves the original
frequency information without distortion.

70
00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:58.202
Reconstructing a continuous signal from
discrete samples requires careful workflow

71
00:06:58.202 --> 00:06:58.905
planning.

72
00:06:58.905 --> 00:07:06.673
First, we identify the signal's maximum
frequency to determine its bandwidth.

73
00:07:06.673 --> 00:07:16.104
Next, we apply an analog low-pass filter to
remove frequencies above half the sampling

74
00:07:16.104 --> 00:07:18.798
rate, preventing spectral overlap.

75
00:07:18.798 --> 00:07:26.441
We then select a sample rate with sufficient
margin to avoid aliasing entirely.

76
00:07:26.441 --> 00:07:34.658
Finally, we verify that the reconstruction
process accurately recovers the original

77
00:07:34.658 --> 00:07:35.405
waveform.

78
00:07:35.405 --> 00:07:43.774
Increasing the sample rate after aliasing has
occurred cannot repair the lost information,

79
00:07:43.774 --> 00:07:48.280
as the overlapping frequencies are permanently
mixed.

80
00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:56.399
This systematic approach ensures digital
systems faithfully represent analog signals

81
00:07:56.399 --> 00:08:00.459
for further processing or storage.

82
00:08:00.602 --> 00:08:07.980
Let's trace a single sinusoidal signal through
a practical filter and sampler.

83
00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:13.016
The input is a pure tone at a specific
frequency.

84
00:08:13.016 --> 00:08:20.427
The filter first shapes the spectrum,
attenuating unwanted frequencies while

85
00:08:20.427 --> 00:08:23.391
preserving the target tone.

86
00:08:23.391 --> 00:08:30.373
The sampler then captures this filtered signal
at discrete time points.

87
00:08:30.373 --> 00:08:38.384
If the tone's frequency falls within the
passband and below the Nyquist limit, the

88
00:08:38.384 --> 00:08:42.962
sampled values will accurately represent the
original wave.

89
00:08:42.962 --> 00:08:50.534
We can verify this by reconstructing the
samples and comparing them to the input.

90
00:08:50.534 --> 00:08:58.675
This end-to-end trace demonstrates how
filtering and sampling work together to

91
00:08:58.675 --> 00:09:03.855
preserve frequency information while enabling
digital processing.

92
00:09:03.855 --> 00:09:11.955
Engineers use this principle to design
communication systems that transmit audio or

93
00:09:11.955 --> 00:09:13.980
data without distortion.

94
00:09:14.138 --> 00:09:23.000
A fundamental principle in signals and systems
is that equivalent time- and frequency-domain

95
00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.409
views must produce consistent predictions.

96
00:09:26.409 --> 00:09:33.820
When we analyze a system using differential
equations in the time domain, we should get

97
00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:39.748
the same output as when we use convolution
with the impulse response.

98
00:09:39.748 --> 00:09:47.248
Similarly, frequency-domain multiplication
should match time-domain results.

99
00:09:47.248 --> 00:09:55.361
This consistency allows engineers to switch
between domains based on which is

100
00:09:55.361 --> 00:09:56.713
mathematically simpler.

101
00:09:56.713 --> 00:10:05.453
For example, we might solve a complex circuit
using Laplace transforms in the frequency

102
00:10:05.453 --> 00:10:11.695
domain, then verify the results by simulating
the time-domain response.

103
00:10:11.695 --> 00:10:19.206
This cross-validation ensures our models
accurately capture physical behavior and

104
00:10:19.206 --> 00:10:23.713
prevents design errors in real-world
applications.

105
00:10:23.858 --> 00:10:29.700
To apply these concepts, we use a practical
analysis checklist.

106
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:37.546
First, identify whether the signal is
continuous or discrete and determine its

107
00:10:37.546 --> 00:10:38.200
bandwidth.

108
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:45.218
Next, model the system as LTI and obtain its
impulse response.

109
00:10:45.218 --> 00:10:52.825
Verify that the signal frequency falls within
the passband and below the Nyquist rate.

110
00:10:52.825 --> 00:11:01.308
Apply anti-aliasing filtering if necessary,
then select a sample rate with margin.

111
00:11:01.308 --> 00:11:09.383
Finally, trace the signal through the filter
and sampler, checking that reconstructed

112
00:11:09.383 --> 00:11:12.075
values match the original.

113
00:11:12.075 --> 00:11:21.264
This checklist ensures systematic design,
prevents aliasing, and validates frequency

114
00:11:21.264 --> 00:11:22.183
preservation.

115
00:11:22.183 --> 00:11:30.423
By following these steps, engineers can
confidently build reliable signal processing

116
00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:36.415
systems that meet performance requirements
across diverse applications.
